\b PyroSense Python Machine Learning Explanation\b0\par
\par
\b What this document is for\b0\par
This file explains in simple terms how the Python machine learning part of the project works, what model is being used, and how Laravel communicates with it.\par
\par
\b Short answer\b0\par
Yes. This project uses a real machine learning model in Python. The model is trained in `capstone-ai/train_model.py` and used for prediction in `capstone-ai/predict_event.py`.\par
\par
\b Files involved\b0\par
`capstone-ai/train_model.py`: trains the machine learning model.\par
`capstone-ai/predict_event.py`: loads the trained model and predicts risk.\par
`capstone-ai/fire_model.pkl`: saved trained model file.\par
`capstone-ai/fire_model_metadata.json`: saved metadata about the model.\par
`app/Services/BarangayPredictionService.php`: Laravel service that runs the Python script.\par
`app/Services/ForecastModelService.php`: checks whether the model needs retraining.\par
\par
\b What model is used\b0\par
The training code uses scikit-learn models. In the current code, the main imported models are `RandomForestClassifier`, `RandomForestRegressor`, and `CalibratedClassifierCV`.\par
\par
This means the project is not just using normal Python rules or if-statements. It is using a trained machine learning model saved into a `.pkl` file.\par
\par
\b Python libraries used\b0\par
`pandas`: for preparing tabular data.\par
`joblib`: for saving and loading the trained model.\par
`scikit-learn`: for the machine learning model and preprocessing pipeline.\par
\par
\b How training works\b0\par
The `train_model.py` script reads historical fire-related data, prepares the features, trains the machine learning model, and saves the result into `capstone-ai/fire_model.pkl`.\par
\par
The model metadata is also saved into `capstone-ai/fire_model_metadata.json` so the application can track feature columns and model information.\par
\par
\b How prediction works\b0\par
When Laravel needs a prediction, it builds a payload for a station and barangay, then runs the Python prediction script.\par
\par
The Python script loads the saved model, transforms the input into the expected feature format, and returns prediction results as JSON.\par
\par
\b How Laravel communicates with Python\b0\par
Laravel does not currently call Python through a separate HTTP API service. Instead, Laravel runs the Python script directly as a local process on the same server.\par
\par
This happens inside `app/Services/BarangayPredictionService.php` using Laravel `Process::run(...)`. So Python still executes, but you do not manually run it for normal app usage.\par
\par
\b Does this count as machine learning\b0\par
Yes. Because the project trains a model from historical data and saves that trained model for later predictions, this is a machine learning workflow.\par
\par
\b Deployment note\b0\par
If you deploy this project, the server must have Python installed together with the required ML libraries such as `pandas`, `joblib`, and `scikit-learn`. Since Laravel runs the Python script locally, both PHP and Python need to be available in the deployed environment.\par
